\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}
\paragraph{Leaderboards and their statistics.}
Chatbot Arena aggregates crowd votes on anonymous model pairs into Bradley--Terry (BT) scores~\citep{bradley1952} with bootstrap confidence intervals~\citep{chiang2024}; MT-Bench provides expert and GPT-4 judgments of the same answer pairs~\citep{zheng2023}. Subsequent work asks how the \emph{collection} of Arena data distorts the board~\citep{singh2025}, proposes richer statistical models for ties and covariance~\citep{ameli2024}, and combines human with LLM-judge comparisons to rank with valid uncertainty~\citep{chatzi2024}. Inference for ranks of BT items is developed by \citet{liu2023}. These works address sampling noise or collection bias; we ask how much of the \emph{given} comparison data carries a published conclusion, on a scale that can be compared across datasets.

\paragraph{Pairwise-comparison models.}
We use BT maximum likelihood, fitted by minorisation--maximisation~\citep{hunter2004}, with ties counted as half a win as Arena does. Alternatives (spectral and regularised estimators~\citep{negahban2017,chen2019}; \citet{elo1978} as online BT~\citep{kiraly2017}; explicit tie models~\citep{rao1967,davidson1970}) are not the object of study here; the top-$K$ identifiability theory of \citet{chen2019} motivates the $\sqrt N$ scaling we use.

\paragraph{Influence and robustness to data changes.}
Influence curves and functions~\citep{hampel1974,cook1977,koh2017} and the approximate maximum influence perturbation of Broderick et al.~\citeyearpar{broderick2020} ask which few data points drive a conclusion; our search applies that idea to a ranking conclusion and verifies it by exact refitting. Rank-list sensitivity to interaction perturbations has been studied for recommenders~\citep{oh2022rls}.

\paragraph{Rigging and poisoning.}
Min et al.\ study vote-rigging strategies on Arena, whose inserted votes pass through the platform's sampler~\citeyearpar{min2025}. Attacks on pairwise rank aggregation more generally~\citep{ma2022poison,ma2022hodge,ma2024seq}, and top-$K$ ranking with an adversary who adds edges~\citep{yang2024monotone}, design or bound the alteration for a given system. We study deletions and outcome flips of existing comparisons as measures of data dependence, not an attack; numbers are comparable with sampler-constrained attacks only under a matched threat model. A paper on dropping a handful of preferences from Arena-style rankings~\citep{huang2025} is the closest to our counts of deleted comparisons. We chose not to read it, to keep this study independent of it (Appendix~\ref{app:literature}); readers should compare the two, and we state no priority over it.
